<section class="hero"><div class="eyebrow">ANTICIPATE ORIGINAL RESEARCH / THE MODEL ROOM</div>
<h1>Tyrese Haliburton: A Career-Ending Injury? Or a <em>Career-Defining One?</em></h1>
<p class="dek">A torn Achilles can change everything. But how much does it really change the player who comes back?</p>
<div class="meta"><span>NBA ANALYTICS</span><span>2026–27 SEASON</span><span>24 HISTORICAL CASES</span><span>OCTOBER 2026</span></div>
<figure class="hero-image"><img src="https://images.assettype.com/tribune/2025-06-23/dosvozbv/0-02-06-0c75b444bcd99a2496a22a001bbb3212dd96418968c09ed0ad3ec66215837d6c2724db3388f0a04c.jpg?w=1200&amp;auto=format" alt="Tyrese Haliburton at the 2025 NBA Finals" fetchpriority="high"/>
<figcaption class="caption">Tyrese Haliburton during the 2025 NBA Finals. Image hosted by <a href="https://www.tribuneindia.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Tribune</a> / Assettype. Photographer and reuse rights are not established by this credit; confirm permission before public commercial publication.</figcaption></figure>
</section>
<section class="content"><article class="prose">
<p class="opening">When Tyrese Haliburton went down in Game 7 of the Finals, it was hard not to wonder if we'd ever see the same player again.</p>
<p>A torn Achilles has that kind of reputation in basketball. It's an injury that can change a career, and even when a player makes it back, nobody really knows how much of their old game they'll have left.</p>
<p>Haliburton tore his right Achilles in Game 7 of the 2025 NBA Finals and missed the following season. What stuck with me was how quickly the conversation changed. One minute people were talking about the Pacers' Finals run, and the next they were wondering what kind of player Haliburton would be when he returned.</p>
<p>I've always found his game interesting because he doesn't need to be the fastest or strongest guy on the floor. He reads defenses, finds his teammates and seems to know where the ball should go before anyone else does. I wondered whether that could make his return a little different, so I started looking at what happened to other players after the same injury.</p>
<h2>Before the injury</h2>
<p>Haliburton averaged 18.6 points and 9.2 assists in the 2024–25 regular season, shooting 38.8% from three. What stands out to me is how much he does for the players around him. He can have a great game without needing to take every shot.</p>
<div class="stats"><div class="stat"><strong>18.6</strong><span>POINTS PER GAME<br/>2024–25</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>9.2</strong><span>ASSISTS PER GAME<br/>2024–25</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>38.8%</strong><span>THREE-POINT SHOOTING<br/>2024–25</span></div></div>
<p>I didn't want to compare players only by their points per game, since someone coming back from an injury might be playing fewer minutes. So for this project, I looked at their numbers <strong>per 36 minutes</strong>. It's not a perfect comparison, but it helps.</p>
<h2>What can NBA history tell us?</h2>
<p>The first names people bring up are usually Kevin Durant and Kobe Bryant. Durant returned looking like himself. Kobe's comeback was much harder. Both stories are real, but neither one tells us what will happen to Haliburton. That's why I wanted to look beyond the famous examples.</p>
<p>I ended up with <strong>24 NBA players</strong> in my dataset. I looked at their age, position, size, how long they took to return and what their numbers looked like before and after the injury. Twenty-four players isn't enough to give a definite answer, but I thought it was enough to start noticing some patterns.</p>
<div class="chart"><h3>01 / Before and after the Achilles</h3><small>Historical sample means · production per 36 minutes · n = 24</small>
{statPairs.map(s=><div class="bar-row"><label>{s.label}</label><div class="bar-pair"><div class="bar before" style={`width:${s.before/22*100}%`}>{s.before.toFixed(1)}</div><div class="bar after" style={`width:${s.after/22*100}%`}>{s.after.toFixed(1)}</div></div></div>)}
<div class="legend"><span><i class="swatch" style="background:#85897b"></i>Before injury</span><span><i class="swatch" style="background:#d4ff45"></i>After injury</span></div>
<p class="chart-note">Source: <a href="https://github.com/mudia-OE/Hali-Predict/blob/main/achilles_dataset.csv">Hali Predict historical dataset</a>. These are unweighted averages across players, not season-level league averages.</p></div>
<p>The biggest change was in scoring. Players went from about <strong>19.9 to 17.2 points per 36 minutes</strong> on average. Assists barely changed, staying around 3.9, and rebounds were almost the same too. That doesn't mean passing is unaffected by the injury, but it was interesting to see scoring drop more than the other two stats.</p>
<h2>Looking for patterns in the numbers</h2>
<p>I also wanted to see whether younger players had an easier time coming back. I expected the answer to be yes, but the data wasn't that clear.</p>
<div class="chart"><h3>02 / Does age predict a scoring decline?</h3><small>Age at injury versus post-minus-pre points per 36 · each dot represents one player</small>
<svg class="scatter" viewBox="0 0 690 300" role="img" aria-label="Scatter plot of 24 NBA players: age at injury against change in points per 36 after Achilles injury. The relationship is weak.">
{[-10,-5,0,5].map(t=><g><line x1="58" y1={245-(t+11)/16*190} x2="625" y2={245-(t+11)/16*190} stroke={t===0?"#d4ff45":"#45483e"} stroke-dasharray={t===0?"5 5":"2 6"}/><text x="48" y={249-(t+11)/16*190} text-anchor="end" fill="#b8b9af" font-size="12">{t>0?"+":""}{t}</text></g>)}
{[20,25,30,35].map(t=><g><text x={58+(t-20)/18*565} y="270" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b8b9af" font-size="12">{t}</text></g>)}
{plot.map(p=><circle cx={p.x} cy={p.y} r="5.5" fill="#d4ff45" opacity=".85"><title>{p.name}: age {p.age}, {p.change>0?"+":""}{p.change} points/36</title></circle>)}
<text x="340" y="295" text-anchor="middle" fill="#d4d5ca" font-size="13">Age at injury</text><text x="13" y="145" transform="rotate(-90 13 145)" text-anchor="middle" fill="#d4d5ca" font-size="12">Change in points / 36</text>
</svg><p class="chart-note">Pearson correlation between age and scoring change: <strong>r ≈ −0.11</strong>. That is a very weak negative relationship in this sample, not proof that age has no effect. Hover over dots for player names.</p></div>
<p>Some younger players struggled, while a few older players came back and played well. The correlation was very weak, so I couldn't really say age explained much in this group. Haliburton being 25 might help, but it doesn't guarantee anything.</p>
<h2>Building Hali Predict</h2>
<p>I wanted to try making an actual prediction instead of just comparing players, so I built <a href="https://github.com/mudia-OE/Hali-Predict">Hali Predict</a>. It's a Python model that looks at how players' numbers changed after an Achilles tear. I used <strong>ridge regression</strong> because I was working with a small dataset and didn't want to make the model more complicated than it needed to be.</p>
<p>I gave it each player's age, height, weight, position, recovery time and pre-injury stats. It then tried to predict <strong>how much their numbers would change</strong>, rather than treating every player like they started at the same level.</p>
<p>To see how well it worked, I tested it on each player one at a time, training it on the other 23 first. This is called <strong>leave-one-out cross-validation</strong>. I wanted to see how close it could get without already knowing the answer.</p>
<div class="method"><strong>MODEL VALIDATION / MEAN ABSOLUTE ERROR</strong><p>Points: <strong>2.70</strong> per 36 · Assists: <strong>0.94</strong> per 36 · Rebounds: <strong>1.37</strong> per 36. These are average absolute errors on held-out historical cases, not guarantees for Haliburton.</p></div>
<h2>What does the model project?</h2>
<p>Using Haliburton's specified pre-injury baseline of 25.3 points, 10.2 assists and 3.9 rebounds per 36, together with a 14-month recovery assumption, the model produces the following central estimates:</p>
<div class="forecast"><div class="stat"><strong>23.4</strong><span>PROJECTED POINTS / 36<br/>Illustrative range: 19.0–27.8</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>10.1</strong><span>PROJECTED ASSISTS / 36<br/>Illustrative range: 8.8–11.4</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>4.4</strong><span>PROJECTED REBOUNDS / 36<br/>Illustrative range: 2.2–6.6</span></div></div>
<p>The assist prediction was the one that interested me most. The model has him at 10.1 assists per 36, almost the same as before. It predicts a small drop in scoring, but nothing that would completely change his game. Considering how important passing is to Haliburton, I thought that was a pretty encouraging result.</p>
<p>One important detail: these are <strong>per-36-minute numbers</strong>, not what I'm predicting he'll average every night. If he's on a minutes restriction, his actual box scores could look very different.</p>
<h2>What the model cannot tell us</h2>
<p>There are some big catches. Twenty-four players is not a lot, especially when you're mixing different eras and different kinds of players. The numbers also depend on which seasons count as 'before' and 'after.' And even if a player's per-36 stats look good, that doesn't tell us whether he's moving the same way, defending as well or staying healthy enough to play a full season.</p>
<p>Those ranges around the predictions come from how far off the model was in its historical tests. They're there to show how uncertain this really is. They are <strong>not guaranteed 80% prediction intervals</strong>, and this isn't a medical prediction about Haliburton's recovery.</p>
<p>I don't expect the model to get everything right. I just wanted a prediction I could actually compare with what happens when he returns.</p>
<h2>The Achilles heel</h2>
<p>When people talk about Achilles, they remember his heel. When people talk about a player tearing his Achilles, it's easy to let that one injury overshadow everything he did before it. I don't think that's fair to the player, and I don't think the numbers support it either.</p>
<p>Some players never got back to where they were. Others changed how they played. And a few came back looking almost impossible to slow down. That's what makes Haliburton's return worth watching.</p>
<p>I don't know if Haliburton will come back as exactly the same player. He might have to change parts of his game, and his numbers might not look the same right away. But after looking at these players, I don't think it's fair to assume his best basketball is already behind him. I'm looking forward to seeing how close the prediction gets.</p>
</article>
